# The Fall of Icarus: where is he?
subtitle: "Bruegel's Fall of Icarus makes the myth's hero the smallest thing in the picture, and nobody in it looks up. Here is why."
tags: ["greek mythology","bruegel painting","perspective and composition","classical literature","art interpretation"]
teaches: ["art-history/bruegel-technique","literature/ovid-mythology","concepts/visual-irony","analysis/composition-and-focus"]
thumb: "band \"Where's Icarus?\" picture \"File:Pieter Bruegel the Elder - Landscape with the Fall of Icarus - Brussels, Royal Museums of Fine Arts of Belgium - Google Arts & Culture.jpg\" colour"
voices: {"a":"gemini:Charon | a warm, unhurried gallery guide, quietly amused"}

## The Fall of Icarus: where is he?
sources:
    - id: rmfab
      title: Landscape with the Fall of Icarus
      authors: Royal Museums of Fine Arts of Belgium, Brussels
      year: 2024
      finding: Probably a copy of a lost Bruegel original; the composition is certainly his
    - id: ovid
      title: Metamorphoses, Book VIII
      authors: Ovid
      year: 8
      finding: Daedalus and Icarus; the angler, shepherd and ploughman take them for gods; Perdix turned into a partridge

    card title "Landscape with the Fall of Icarus"

This is Landscape with the Fall of Icarus, based on a design by Pieter Bruegel from the late fifteen-fifties.
    image art cites ["rmfab"] credit "After Bruegel, c. 1560. Brussels. Public domain." x 500 y 335 width 900 url https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Pieter_Bruegel_the_Elder_-_Landscape_with_the_Fall_of_Icarus_-_Brussels%2C_Royal_Museums_of_Fine_Arts_of_Belgium_-_Google_Arts_%26_Culture.jpg/1920px-Pieter_Bruegel_the_Elder_-_Landscape_with_the_Fall_of_Icarus_-_Brussels%2C_Royal_Museums_of_Fine_Arts_of_Belgium_-_Google_Arts_%26_Culture.jpg look screen regions.angler [0.84,0.89,0.045,0.085] regions.bird [0.802,0.835,0.034,0.045] regions.corner [0.76,0.7,0.14,0.3] regions.legs [0.835,0.722,0.042,0.052] regions.plough_team [0.08,0.51,0.41,0.36] regions.ploughman [0.36,0.51,0.13,0.36] regions.shepherd [0.49,0.5,0.04,0.17] regions.ship [0.672,0.3,0.21,0.39] regions.sky [0,0,1,0.25] regions.sun [0.64,0.24,0.032,0.035]

Bruegel was a Flemish painter, best known for crowded scenes of country people at work.
    portrait bruegel of "Pieter Bruegel the Elder" cameo true

    erase bruegel

In the Greek myth, Icarus escapes from Crete on wings his father Daedalus made of feathers and wax.
    highlight art:sky strength soft

He flies too close to the sun, the wax melts, and he falls into the sea.
    highlight art:sun

    ask "Where is Icarus? Tap him." spot legs on art
        intro: So the title promises a dramatic fall. But where is it?
        right: There he is: two legs, kicking in the water.
        wrong: Look at the sea, just below and to the right of the big ship.

    camera on ["art@[0.66,0.42,0.28,0.4]"]

That's the hero of the title: two legs by the big ship, already going under.
    highlight art:legs strength spotlight rest grey lift true

    camera reset true

Meanwhile, the biggest figure in the whole picture is a ploughman.
    highlight art:ploughman lift true

He comes from Ovid, the Roman poet whose telling of the myth names three people wh (@highlight art:ploughman art:shepherd art:angler@)o saw them fly: a ploughman, a shepherd and an angler.

In Ovid, all three look up in astonishment, taking the flyers for gods.
    highlight art:sky strength soft

    camera on ["art:plough_team"]

Bruegel keeps all three, but changes what they do. The ploughman keeps his eyes down on his furrow.
    highlight art:ploughman

    camera on ["art@[0.36,0.12,0.3,0.55]"]

The shepherd does look up, but at the empty sky, turned away from the sea.
    highlight art:shepherd

    camera on ["art:corner"]

And the angler, closest of all, watches only his line.
    highlight art:angler

    camera reset true

Even the great ship sails on past the splash.
    highlight art:ship

    camera on ["art:corner"]

One creature does seem to look: a bird on a branch, often read as a partridge.
    highlight art:bird strength strong rest blur lift true

In Ovid, Daedalus once tried to kill his gifted nephew, and the goddess Minerva saved the boy by turning him into a partridge.
    highlight art:bird lift true

So some read the bird as that nephew, watching his old attacker lose a son.
    highlight art:bird art:legs

One more twist: this canvas in Brussels is probably a copy of a lost original, though the design is Bruegel's.
    camera reset true

Make the hero tiny and the ploughman huge, and the myth turns around.
    highlight art:ploughman art:legs lift true

    camera on ["art:corner"]

A boy's death becomes a splash at the edge of an ordinary working day.
    highlight art:legs rest paper lift true

    camera reset true

The tragedy is not only that I (@highlight art:ploughman art:shepherd art:angler lift true@)carus falls. It's that nobody looks.

    quiz "A painter wants to show the cost of a war the way Bruegel shows Icarus. Which picture follows his method?"
        right: The family at supper: everyday life in front, the disaster small and far off, and nobody looking.
        wrong: Two things at once: the disaster is small and far off, and the people in front do not look.
        * A tiny burning village alone in an empty landscape
        + A family at supper while, far off, a village burns
        * Villagers at supper, all turning to stare at a distant fire
        * A weeping general, close up

And you did what nobody in the painting did: you went looking for him.
    highlight art:legs lift true
---
